488: The orientations are the same two reversals as K7. The complex
489: orientation on \(W\) has outer boundary \(F\times S^1_\rho\) and
490: inner boundary the negative of the complex-domain link. The outer
491: boundary of \(Y\times D_\rho\) is \(-P_f\), and the excised inner
492: seven-ball supplies \(-N_e\). The remaining normal-sphere boundary
493: has zero index. Hence
494: \[
495:                          A(P_f)=-A(N_e).
496: \]
497: The actual positive family is Jin's mapping torus for \(f^{-1}\), so
498: \[
499:                          A_{\rm Jin}(f)=A(N_e).      \tag{SC.17}
500: \]
501: 
502: For an actual Seifert embedding of the lifted link in its six-sphere,
503: repeat the identical normal-tube excision, cap its boundary by \(D^4\),
504: and use the complex normal two-plane plus the radial real line. The
505: meridian line on the surgery boundary is the one in (SC.14); on the
506: three-sphere a line or its gauge has no additional class. The sphere-
507: bundle formula gives
508: \[
509:  A(N_e)=\frac{e(\nu V)^2-\sigma(V)}8
510:                       =H(e),                        \tag{SC.18}
511: \]
512: where the last equality is Takase's corrected Haefliger formula,
513: recorded in P3F.5. The normalization comparison with the invariant
514: computed directly in P3F.55 is its equations (CL.16)--(CL.19): an
515: actual punctured Kummer surface embedded in the oriented five-sphere
516: has signature \(-16\), trivial normal Euler class and value two
517: for both invariants. The exact Whitehead-to-Stiefel morphism and the
518: sign-refined immersed Seifert formula give that equality on this
519: nonzero element. The two group isomorphisms to \(\mathbb Z\)
520: therefore agree on every embedding. This supplies the sign bridge
521: used here.
522: 
523: There is no unrecorded mirror sign in this comparison. In the local
524: coordinates of F9 the circle-first orbit orientation is
525: \(-\Omega_{\rm source}\wedge d\gamma\). Thus the local seven-ball
526: orientation is the negative of complex \((X,y,\rho)\) followed by the
527: increasing height. The positive Hopf meridian is reversed at the same
528: time relative to that raw complex-plus-height normal orientation.
529: Changing both the six-dimensional orientation and the Euler class
530: leaves \((4x^3-p_1x)/24\) unchanged. This gives (SC.18) with the
531: positive height ordering in SC.3 and the positive \(\rho\)-loop of
532: F54.
533: 
534: The direct local calculation P3F.55, with that exact normalization
535: bridge, gives \(H(e)=1\). Equations (SC.17)--(SC.18), after the actual
536: line and collar maps above, therefore give
537: \[
538:  A_{\rm Jin}(f)=1,\qquad [\Phi]=1\in\Theta_7\cong\mathbb Z/28.
539:                                                                \tag{SC.19}
540: \]
